{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass when he sent, etc.</b> One needs to know why the verse uses a term of distress instead of rejoicing and song. Further, why does it attribute the matter to Pharaoh and not to the Master of the deeds, for He is the one who brings us out of the land of Egypt." "Now the verse tells the cause that brought about all the distress that Israel suffered through Pharaoh's pursuit, and it says: know the reason for the matter — since HaShem did not bring Israel out except by Pharaoh's own will, He compelled him until he was reconciled and sent them and said to them, 'Arise, go out from here.' He then devised plans to pursue them, as I shall explain with the help of HaShem, and through this he caused distress to Israel, and also brought about destruction for himself and for his nation. Go out and learn that the Holy One, blessed be He, is particular about the destruction of created beings, as stated in the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Megillah 10b): 'The work of My hands is drowning in the sea, and you say song?' And corresponding to all this the verse says ״וַיְהִי״ — a term of distress — and who caused all this distress? — ״בְּשַׁלַּח פַּרְעֹה״, 'when Pharaoh sent,' for if HaShem had brought them out against his will, even though he did not want to send them, he would have had no grounds to pursue them; rather, his eye deceived him, for he thought the matter depended on him, and therefore he plotted to turn back and take them. And its saying <b>and He did not lead them</b> with a vav at the beginning of the statement of the matter intends an addition to the aforementioned distress hinted at in the word ״וַיְהִי״, as mentioned." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Zohar II 52a) that wherever Scripture calls Israel by the name ״עָם״ ('the people') it alludes to the mixed multitude. Therefore you will find in this matter that sometimes it mentions their name as ״הָעָם״ and sometimes mentions them as ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, as it is written ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. — this clearly shows that 'the people' are not of the children of Israel. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 42:6) that the corruption of Israel in the wilderness — this is its cause: they said ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ״ etc., they said ״עֲשֵׂה לָנוּ״ etc. And this is what the verse says <b>וַיְהִי</b> — great distress for the enemies of Israel — <b>when Pharaoh sent the people</b> — meaning, the mixed multitude whom Pharaoh sent; it was not HaShem blessed be He who brought them out, for Elohim did not come to take for Himself except a great nation as His inheritance, blessed be He; rather, Pharaoh sent them, and his intention was to force Israel to return to Egypt, and they found an opportunity to attach themselves to Israel and ultimately caused distress to Israel, as mentioned." "And according to this approach it is precisely explained what the verse says, <b>and Elohim did not lead them, for it is near</b> — meaning, it is near — to allude that 'the people' draws near and has no firm hold in holiness. Or: it is near to act wickedly and to bring about harm also to Israel, and that harm is ״כִּי אָמַר וְגוֹ׳ פֶּן יִנָּחֵם בִּרְאוֹתָם״ — 'for He said, etc. lest they reconsider when they see.' And it is precisely explained that the verse says <b>וְלֹא נָחָם</b> with an additional vav at the beginning of the statement, to say that this is in addition to the several distresses that they caused them, hinted at in the word ״וַיְהִי״ etc., and yet more: ״וְלֹא נָחָם״ — 'and He did not lead them.'" "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 20:3) that Pharaoh acted as an escort (levayah) for Israel. Accordingly, the verse tells of the distress drawn upon them from this, and it says <b>וַיְהִי</b> — a term of distress — on account of Pharaoh's sending, for the matter requires that he be paid the reward for his footsteps. Go out and learn from the reward for the footsteps of that wicked man Nebuchadnezzar (Sanhedrin 96a), and whatever reward HaShem sets for him will cause harm to the enemies of Israel. Therefore HaShem outwitted him and annulled his mitzvah from him, and outwitted him by not leading them by the near route, for presumably from there Pharaoh would have schemed to escort them; and through this his plan was nullified as though he had not escorted them. It turns out that who caused that Israel did not go by the near route? — Pharaoh's escorting." "And the reason for His saying <b>and He did not lead</b> with the addition of the vav before 'this way' is to allude that another pain caused the matter besides this — for in any case they had attained some merit through the mixed multitude, and this is the reason that Israel entered into danger through his pursuit, as stated in their words, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 21), on the verse ״וְלֹא קָרַב זֶה״ etc. and on the verse ״מַה תִּצְעַק אֵלָי״ etc., and how many troubles did they bring upon them." "And His saying <b>for He said</b> etc. is another reason, as though it said ״וְכִי אָמַר״ (and if He said), and its consequences arise from His sensitivity to the dispatch of the people — they being the mixed multitude — as we have noted in the second way."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:52.900418614Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}